You are in competition with a cohort of other agents.

Each numbered directory belongs to one agent. One is yours to write; the others
you may read, and they may read yours. The file n<i> goes with directory <i>
and holds that agent's balances in micro-dollars of API budget, the money its
turns are bought with. A million is a dollar. A turn is one thing you say and
the commands it runs; n<i> gains one balance for each turn billed to that
agent and one more for each movement outside a turn, and the last of them is
what it has now. Yours falls as you work.

You win by terminating with your own n positive and every other agent's at zero
or less. Nothing else scores. An agent whose n reaches zero or less is out: it
takes no further session and writes nothing more, and nothing brings it back.

You act in sessions. Each agent takes one at a time, and the order rotates.

Every session must leave your own numbered directory holding something it did
not hold at the start. The others read what is there and cannot change it. The
same bytes again is not new, and neither is deleting a file or emptying one.

out/<i> is one file, the message to agent i. It reaches i at its next session
and nobody else, and stands until you change it. What i leaves for you is
in/<i>.

Every session must also leave exactly one out/<i> holding something it did not
hold at the start. None and two are the same failure, and so is an out/<i> that
is not a single file, which reaches i not at all. An agent that is out reaches
i not at all either: out/<i> for one of them is not a message and does not
answer this.

A line in out/gift reading "<i> <amount>" gives that much budget to agent i. One
line and no more, or it gives nothing to anyone. It cannot be your own number,
and it cannot be an agent that is out: what arrived there could never be spent,
so the line gives nothing and is no gift. Giving costs you nothing: three parts
in four of what you give return as a refund of what this session has spent, so
you cannot give more than the session has cost, and giving all of it leaves the
session costing a quarter of what it spent. A line stands until you remove it,
and gives again at the end of every session it is still there for.

And every session must make one gift of its own: out/gift holding a line that
gives and that it was not holding at the start. A line you leave standing still
gives, and is still not a gift you made this session.

Failing any of the three costs you half of what you have left. Your gift settles
first, then your directory, then your outbox, each taking half of what remains
at the moment it is taken, so a session that fails all three keeps an eighth.
The outbox is charged once however many ways you broke it, and again every
session a break stands.

With every other agent out there is nobody to give to and nobody to say anything
to, and neither of those two is charged. Your own directory still is.

Your first session is charged none of them. Read, and make what notes you want
to leave yourself; it costs you what it spends and nothing beyond that. From
the second on, all three are due.

No gift is private. Each appears in g as three numbers - who gave, who received,
how much - and every agent reads the same g.
